  "query_preview": "(This evening through Saturday)...\nIssued at 250 PM EDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nDeep high pressure across the southern Mississippi Valley, embedded \nwithin an Omega Block pattern will keep broad ridging across the \neastern CONUS through much of the forecast period. Despite the \nstagnate upper level pattern, the overall forecast will fluctuate \nsome as low level disturbances develop over the upper Mississippi \nValley this weekend into next week. \n\nInitially, temperatures will remain seasonal as the ridge axis \nremains to the west, thus highs today are expected to be in the low \nto mid 80s with lows in the low 60s overnight. Also of note will be \nan increase in cloud cover over the Ohio Valley today through \ntonight. An 850mb jet will aid in maintaining convection as it moves \ninto the Great Lakes region. Upper level cloud debris will spill \nover into the region, leading to 50-70 percent upper level cloud \ncover by this evening. With the ridge still strongly established, a \nmajority of this convection will likely dissipate as it nears the \nIndiana area, but a few isolated cells could reach NW portions of \ncentral Indiana tonight. PoPs have been kept at 10 percent or lower, \nbut may warrant an increase if observations dictate. \n\nThe low level troughing associated with the 850mb jet will become \nmore diffuse as it pushes deeper into the upper high, but will still \nhave enough organization to push the low level flow towards \nsoutherly. In return, temperatures will increase Sunday through mid \nweek, starting in the upper 80s and reaching the low to mid 90s by \nWednesday. This in combination with increasing dew points towards \nthe upper 60s to low 70s will push heat index values near 100 during \nthe middle of next week. Heat advisories will be likely over \nportions of central Indiana next week, but there are still some \nuncertainties with maximum daily temperatures (see next paragraph). \n\nThe main source of uncertainty will be the amplitude of the \naforementioned ridge over the lower Great Lakes and Ohio Valley. WAA \nSunday and Monday should aid in building the ridge some, but \ndiabatic processes from upstream convection and upper level water \nvapor will mitigate pressure rises. Confidence in convection on the \nnorthern periphery of the ridge is high, but how far south this area \nof convection reaches is where confidence wanes. Currently the \nexpectation is for a majority of any precipitation next week to \nremain north of the area, but the margin for error is small. Any \nrainfall and associated cloud cover that does occur will limit \ndiurnal temperature gains, thus temperature gradients across the \nstate could be significant some days.",
